# Every environment variable the database module (src/db) reads. Nine of
# them: one for transport, four connection URLs, one pool size and three
# timeouts.
#
# Two line shapes, and a test depends on both. A plain `NAME=value` line is
# a variable with no default, so the process will not start without it. A
# `# default: NAME=value` line is a variable the module supplies itself,
# and the value shown is the real default read out of the code; uncomment
# the line only to change it. An empty default names a fallback, described
# above the line.
#
# The test lives in src/db/env-template.test.ts. It fails if a variable is
# added to the module and not to this file, if a variable is listed here
# that nothing in the module reads, if a default printed here stops
# matching the default in the code, or if the placeholder values below stop
# forming a configuration the loader accepts.
#
# Passwords never belong in this file. Fill them from the host's secret
# store at deploy time; REPLACE_ME is a placeholder, not a value.

# How the connection to the server is protected.
#
# `pinned`, the default, verifies the server and its host name against the
# two roots embedded in src/db/tls.ts, the ones Render's Let's Encrypt
# certificates chain to. Nothing else is trusted: not the system trust
# store, not a certificate the URL names, and the URL may not carry an
# ssl parameter at all, because that would replace or disable the pin.
#
# `off` connects with no TLS, and it refuses to do so for anything but a
# loopback host: localhost, 127.0.0.1 or ::1. Point any URL at a real host
# with this set and the process stops at startup rather than sending
# tenant identifiers and payout rows across a network in the clear. It
# exists for a local container and a developer's own Postgres, and it has
# no use in a deployed environment.
# default: UJIRA_DB_TLS=pinned

# The logins, one per role, each with its own pool. The host and database
# name below are examples: take both from the Render database's external
# connection settings. The logins and passwords are the ones provisioning
# made, not Render's default user. Wrong value: the process stops at
# startup, or connects as a role with the wrong grants.
#
# Port matters. 6432 is Render's PgBouncer, a transaction pooler, and 5432
# is the server itself. The worker and the migrator are refused on 6432
# (and on 6543, the earlier host's pooler): a transaction pooler delivers
# no LISTEN notification, so the worker would wait forever, and the
# migration tool holds its advisory lock in the session, so a migration
# would lose the lock between statements.
UJIRA_DB_APP_URL=postgresql://ujira_app:REPLACE_ME@dpg-example-a.virginia-postgres.render.com:6432/ujira_example
UJIRA_DB_WORKER_URL=postgresql://ujira_worker:REPLACE_ME@dpg-example-a.virginia-postgres.render.com:5432/ujira_example
UJIRA_DB_MIGRATOR_URL=postgresql://ujira_migrator:REPLACE_ME@dpg-example-a.virginia-postgres.render.com:5432/ujira_example

# The read only login, for reads across every project of a principal. Left
# empty, the reader pool logs in with UJIRA_DB_APP_URL, which reads under
# the same policies.
# default: UJIRA_DB_READER_URL=

# Connections per process for the app pool and the worker pool, each. The
# reader keeps 2 and the migrator 1. The database allows 100 in total and
# this deployment may use 30 of them, so the sum is checked against that
# and startup fails if it is exceeded. Several instances multiply it.
# default: UJIRA_DB_POOL_SIZE=5

# How long a caller waits for a free connection from the pool before it is
# given an error. Too high and a database stall turns into a queue of held
# requests; too low and an ordinary traffic peak fails requests that would
# have been served.
# default: UJIRA_DB_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Server side limits sent on every transaction. Both must stay under
# 8000 ms, and startup fails if either reaches it, because the app role's
# own server side limits are set under that, and a limit above them is
# never the one that fires.
# default: UJIRA_DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# default: UJIRA_DB_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS=3000
